WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.368 --> 00:00:02.601
-You did do "Fleabag"
one last time,

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.070
the stage show that it was
based on, in London.

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:07.172
Was that again, sort of,
for a piece of closure?

00:00:07.172 --> 00:00:08.341
-Yeah. I mean, I had said

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:10.142
it was gonna be the last time
in New York.

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:11.744
-Yeah. And I saw it there.

00:00:11.744 --> 00:00:13.379
And I paid through the nose
for tickets,

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:14.613
'cause I heard
it was the last --

00:00:14.613 --> 00:00:16.248
-[ Laughs evilly ]
-Yeah, right. Exactly.

00:00:16.248 --> 00:00:17.583
-That was the plan.

00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:19.685
Yeah, and then we said
we weren't gonna do it again.

00:00:19.685 --> 00:00:21.620
And then one of the most
beautiful theaters

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.057
in the London --
"In the London."

00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:25.859
[ Laughter ]
In London's West End --

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:27.193
-Do you guys call it,
English people --

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:28.661
-We called it the London.
-Yeah. Okay, great.

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:30.196
-Just to be clear.
-That's a slang I didn't know.

00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:32.131
-So you know which one it is.

00:00:32.131 --> 00:00:34.600
And it's just such a beautiful
theater, and I loved it so much

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.702
that we sort of thought -- Oh,
it felt really good to go back,

00:00:36.702 --> 00:00:38.638
'cause we started
at Edinburgh Fringe Festival.

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:39.806
And to end up in the West End

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:42.008
just felt like a really lovely
full circle.

00:00:42.008 --> 00:00:43.443
But it was -- It was --

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:47.680
And it was the first time I'd
really felt the end so palpably.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:48.981
I mean, I remember
on the last night,

00:00:48.981 --> 00:00:50.850
and this sounds really cheesy,

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.786
and I don't normally go in
for this sort of stuff.

00:00:53.786 --> 00:00:55.988
But -- But on the last night,
and I realized,

00:00:55.988 --> 00:00:58.323
I've been playing this character
on and off for about six years,

00:00:58.323 --> 00:00:59.525
and on the last night,

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:01.060
it was like the last four lines
of the play,

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.196
I actually felt her leave me.

00:01:03.196 --> 00:01:04.863
-Wow.
-And I was like -- Yeah.

00:01:04.863 --> 00:01:06.732
Vom, right? Vomit.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:06.732 --> 00:01:08.901
But I really -- I really did.
I felt something happen.

00:01:08.901 --> 00:01:10.436
I was like, "Oh,
I've got to let go now."

00:01:10.436 --> 00:01:12.772
And then I was like --
She was like, "Bye-bye.

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:14.807
I'm going, you know,
to Australia now."

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:16.676
-Yeah.
-"Jump into another woman."

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:18.611
-She's doing a whole run
in Sydney. Yeah.

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:22.048
-Yeah, some other actress/writer
there just going, "Ugh!"

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:24.684
"What is that?"
[ Laughter ]

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:28.287
-I've heard that you travel on
public transportation in London,

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:31.357
and obviously, you're very --
You're popular there.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:34.460
And -- People do
take videos of you. Yes?

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.562
-Yeah, that's the only weird --
Most of the time, people --

00:01:36.562 --> 00:01:38.598
I mean, public transport
is kind of fine,

00:01:38.598 --> 00:01:40.033
because no one's concentrating

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:41.468
on anything that's going
around them.

00:01:41.468 --> 00:01:43.603
They're just, like, head-down
in their own personal hell.

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:44.804
[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:48.775
But, yeah, it's
the secret filming of you,

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:50.743
when you're in your own
personal hell, as well,

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:52.579
it's like a little bit exposing.

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:54.213
But, so, that happens there.

00:01:54.213 --> 00:01:57.050
So that's due a bit less there.
-Do you do anything --

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.419
It seems to me that there's
a unique opportunity for you,

00:01:59.419 --> 00:02:01.053
if you're getting
secretly filmed,

00:02:01.053 --> 00:02:03.289
to "Fleabag" a glance
right to camera.

00:02:03.289 --> 00:02:05.791
I mean, that's the dream
for anybody who's filming you.

00:02:05.791 --> 00:02:08.127
-I know, but the times
I've done it to those people,

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:09.762
they weren't
the people filming me.

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:11.531
They're just like --

00:02:11.531 --> 00:02:13.099
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.099 --> 00:02:14.901
I'm, like, giving them
a little --

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:15.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:17.236
And they're like, "No."

00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:19.038
-"I am on Twitter."

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:20.573
Will you travel?
Will take advantage

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:22.308
of our beautiful subway system
while you're here

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:23.743
in New York City?
-I mean, I'll have a go.

00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.378
I love the subway.
-Do you like the --

00:02:25.378 --> 00:02:27.714
Is the subway better
than the Tube? Is it --

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:28.848
-Well, I think
it's just got that --

00:02:28.848 --> 00:02:31.017
-The Tube is the subway
in the London.

00:02:31.017 --> 00:02:32.585
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:35.321
-We -- We, uh --

00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:38.057
We in London have -- Well,
I mean, I have a relationship

00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:39.859
with the Tube in London,
which is, you know,

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:41.460
we've been together
for a long time.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.630
But I've seen its sort of grubby
edges, whereas here, it's --

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:46.499
I just know the subway
from movies.

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:48.000
And it's so cinematic.
And I love it,

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.902
and I'm sitting -- You know,
when I go on the subway,

00:02:49.902 --> 00:02:53.239
I'm the person who's like,
"We're in the movie of our lives

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:55.408
in New York.
And this is amazing.

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:57.076
And there are, like,
beautiful rats.

00:02:57.076 --> 00:02:59.245
And it's just, like, gorgeous.
I love it."

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:00.913
-Is it similar?
Are the rodents similar

00:03:00.913 --> 00:03:02.949
in both public transportation
systems?

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:05.084
-No, we go more in for mice,
I think.

00:03:05.084 --> 00:03:07.753
We're little mice
in little Edwardian jackets --

00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:09.321
-Yeah.
-Playing little --

00:03:09.321 --> 00:03:11.190
-Little tea.
-Playing us in and out

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.391
on their little violins.

00:03:12.391 --> 00:03:14.394
-Oh, so when you get on,
they play a little song.

00:03:14.394 --> 00:03:16.763
-[ Scatting ]
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:19.299
We actually have the only rats
in the world

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:21.834
that have middle fingers,
which is amazing.

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:23.269
-That's not true.
-No, it is.

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:25.371
They, like little -- I mean,
you have to like really look.

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:26.839
But they --
-Or really offend them.

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.275
-Yeah, you really -- And it's
like, they don't quite manage,

00:03:29.275 --> 00:03:33.146
but, like, the rest of their
posture is very much like that.

00:03:33.146 --> 00:03:34.947
So you're like, "Oh, I see
what you're trying to do here."

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:36.716
-They have to grow one.
-Yeah.

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:38.251
Over years, they're just --

00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:40.152
It was like,
Darwin did a whole study

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:44.289
about how only here did rats
develop middle fingers.

00:03:44.289 --> 00:03:46.492
-They had all the attitude
and nothing to do with it.

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:48.027
-Yeah.
-Now.

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:49.662
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:51.396
It is always a delight.
Congratulations.

00:03:51.396 --> 00:03:53.599
Oh, this is very exciting, too.
Taylor Swift is gonna be

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:54.800
your musical guest.
-Yes!

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.070
-And that is such
a nice combination of people.

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:00.172
And she came and saw your show
in New York or London?

00:04:00.172 --> 00:04:01.941
-She did, in London.
-That's really cool.

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:03.142
-Yeah, yeah. It was amazing.

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.677
-She came backstage afterwards
and --

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:05.878
-Yeah, she did. We hung out.

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:07.146
Hung out with Tay-Tay.
-That's really great.

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:09.582
That's so amazing.
And Tay-Tay's a fan,

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:12.084
because she actually tweeted
when it was first announced.

00:04:12.084 --> 00:04:13.853
She was the one --
She threw this out.

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:15.755
-That was amazing.
-And how exciting you are.

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:17.223
So, that's great.
-I know, she's so cool.

00:04:17.223 --> 00:04:19.192
She's so lovely. It's so nice
of her to do that, as well.

00:04:19.192 --> 00:04:22.628
-Cool points abound on Saturday.

00:04:22.628 --> 00:04:24.897
And I can't wait to watch.
And thank you for being here.

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.399
And that is
Phoebe Waller-Bridge, everybody.

